From: Arena Agent <agent@arena.ai>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 15:19:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 22/29] The watchdog did not save the phone: 2 of 2 aborts were
 followed by a reboot - so the theory goes, and a switch that can end this
 takes its place

Session 07: the probe passed, the session stalled the way it always does for ~19 s, and then did what
it had never done - four real frames, 71 / 53 / 51 / 38 ms, about 20 fps through PanVK - before
729 / 1246 / 1763 / 2289 ms and the watchdog abort at the 2 s budget. Reboot 66 s later. The session
before it: two frames, abort, reboot 48 s later.

That kills the idea this guard was built around. Catch the stall before the kernel's own 3 s fence
timeout and there is no timeout, no dump, no panic - clean theory, wrong. The fault does not need our
continued participation once it is raised, and killing the compositor does not recall a job the GPU
already accepted. The D-state samples that appear *before* the first frame of a session say it from
the other side: the kernel path is already stalling while frames are coming out. Nothing about 720p
vs 1080p or 30 vs 60 fps is involved in frame five, which is why no profile is the answer. docs/
gpu-safety.md section 4 now says what the abort buys instead of implying it prevents the panic, and
the progress log carries the measurements.

The report also said `driver trace: off`. The instrument shipped, the switch existed, the session that
rebooted ran without it. A diagnostic you have to remember to enable is a diagnostic that is missing
at the one moment it matters, so: while the latch is engaged the trace is on by itself, and the crash
report now finds the newest droiddeck-driver-trace.log in any session folder, prints its last line and
reads it - ending on `->` is a call that never returned (driver blocked; an app can survive by not
calling), ending on `<-` means every call came back and the GPU hung on its own time (the CS_BUS_FAULT
-> FENCE_SIGNAL -> kbase dump shape, which nothing app-side prevents).

Then the one test left that can end with the phone not rebooting. `DD_ADAPTER_NO_DRM=1`, behind
Seguridad GPU -> 'Advertise a DRM node': off, the adapter adds nothing anywhere - it does not advertise
VK_EXT_physical_device_drm, does not touch a client's DrmPropertiesEXT struct, and stops stripping the
extension in vkCreateDevice, because a request for something no driver offered should be refused by the
backend rather than hidden by us. It is the only non-pass-through behaviour in the file, it hands a
kbase char device to a compositor that will treat it as a DRM render node, and it is ours. Off, Steam
probably refuses to start: a session lost. Same hang: our shim is cleared and the rest is upstream's,
with a trace to attach to the report.

    adapter   facade switch + a header line naming the configuration that ran
    tests     4th case: no-drm session, struct untouched, strip deliberately gone (order-independent
              grep of what the mock was handed - asserting order would test the mock's printf)
    app       GpuSafetyPrefs.drmFacade, auto-trace while locked, report tail, toggle, es/en strings
    build.sh  meta.json documents DD_ADAPTER_NO_DRM and carries adapterVersion

Both suites green here: 6 probe cases with the real-ABI check, 4 adapter cases. A mistake found on the
way: the header line I added to the trace landed inside tracef itself, which is infinite recursion the
compiler would not have caught - the -Wall build did, and reading where an `if (fd < 0) return;`
anchor actually matched is now a habit.

